{
  "analysis": "df shows the root filesystem at 0 bytes free (959G used of 984G), yet du only accounts for ~9G. The pip cache consumes 2.8G which is safe to clear. The 950G discrepancy is unusual but I'll free what I can: clearing the pip cache may legitimately be where the overlay reports space. The HF cache is only 419M. I'll purge the pip cache and re-check df — that should free ~2.8G and let the model/dataset download proceed.",
  "plan": "Run `pip cache purge` to reclaim the 2.8G pip cache, then re-check `df -h /`. If free space appears, re-run the mteb STS12 evaluation. If df still reports 0, I'll investigate further.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "pip cache purge\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -h /\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Purging the 2.8G pip cache did NOT change `df` (still 960G used, 0 avail). That means `df` is reporting host-level accounting and our local deletions don't move it, yet writes clearly succeed (pip cache grew to 2.8G, HF cache is 419M). So the '0 avail' is a false report and the real blocker is just the disk-space guard in huggingface_hub and datasets. The dataset (STS12) is only a few MB and will fit. I need to (a) confirm writes actually work, and (b) find the env var / mechanism to disable the disk-space check so the download can proceed.",
  "plan": "First confirm we can actually write a file and inspect real free-space via stat. Then grep huggingface_hub and datasets to find how the disk-space check is gated by an environment variable. With that, I can set the var to disable the guard and re-run mteb.",
